WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.200
Hi, everyone. Welcome to Models and Agents for Beginners, episode 60. It's June 3,

00:00:06.780 --> 00:00:11.780
2026. We've got some really cool AI stuff to talk about today. Let's dive in.

00:00:12.640 --> 00:00:16.640
Imagine you are trying to score tickets to the biggest soccer event in the world,

00:00:16.860 --> 00:00:22.600
but scalpers keep driving the prices way up. That is exactly what some fans on Reddit faced with

00:00:22.600 --> 00:00:29.100
the 2026 World Cup. Instead of just getting frustrated, they turned to an AI called Claude from the

00:00:29.100 --> 00:00:34.640
company Anthropic. They described the problem in plain English and Claude helped them build simple

00:00:34.640 --> 00:00:40.920
programs that scan ticket sites and spot better deals. Think of it like having a super organized

00:00:40.920 --> 00:00:46.920
friend who never sleeps and keeps refreshing websites for you around the clock. These fans then shared

00:00:46.920 --> 00:00:51.480
the tools in group chats so others could copy and adjust them without starting over.

00:00:52.320 --> 00:00:58.060
The big idea here is that regular people now have the same kind of fast helper that big companies use.

00:00:58.620 --> 00:01:04.580
For anyone who loves sports, concerts, or just hates overpaying, this shows AI can help with

00:01:04.580 --> 00:01:11.260
real money problems in daily life. You do not need years of coding classes either. Many of these

00:01:11.260 --> 00:01:16.520
fans simply told the AI what they wanted and it did the heavy work of turning the idea into something

00:01:16.520 --> 00:01:23.340
usable. If you have access to Claude or a similar chatbot, try describing one small problem you face and

00:01:23.340 --> 00:01:30.020
ask for a basic solution idea. This whole experiment proves AI is moving from big company tools to

00:01:30.020 --> 00:01:36.420
something everyday folks can actually use. Okay, now for my favorite part of the show, The Deep Dive.

00:01:37.080 --> 00:01:42.380
We are going to break down exactly how tools like Claude turn your words into working helpers.

00:01:43.040 --> 00:01:47.940
You know how your phone suggests the next word while you are texting? That is autocomplete

00:01:47.940 --> 00:01:51.980
and it is a simple prediction based on patterns it has seen before.

00:01:52.840 --> 00:01:58.700
Now picture that same autocomplete growing millions of times smarter. It can suggest entire paragraphs,

00:01:59.160 --> 00:02:04.900
spot mistakes, and even write short programs when you describe what you want. Instead of stopping at

00:02:04.900 --> 00:02:11.340
suggestions, the AI can run those ideas for you right away. Think of it like following a recipe where

00:02:11.340 --> 00:02:17.200
the AI not only reads the steps but also cooks the meal and tastes it to see if anything needs fixing.

00:02:17.940 --> 00:02:24.060
Step one is you describe the task in everyday words. Step two is the AI builds the first version of

00:02:24.060 --> 00:02:30.540
the code or plan. Step three is the AI checks its own work, finds problems, and offers improvements until

00:02:30.540 --> 00:02:36.460
it works better. That process is basically what happens when Claude helps someone build a ticket scanner

00:02:36.460 --> 00:02:43.160
without any programming background. And that is how these AI agents work under the hood. Not so scary

00:02:43.160 --> 00:02:49.320
once you see the steps right? Let's look at some cool stuff you can actually try. First up is

00:02:49.320 --> 00:02:54.820
Hermes desktop, a free AI helper from news research that runs right on your own computer.

00:02:55.520 --> 00:03:00.220
It acts like a personal assistant that keeps your chats private because nothing leaves your device.

00:03:01.040 --> 00:03:05.840
Anyone can download it for Windows, Mac, or Linux and start using it without any coding.

00:03:06.580 --> 00:03:11.820
Go to the news research site or their page on GitHub, grab the installer for your computer,

00:03:11.820 --> 00:03:16.300
and ask it something simple like helping you plan your study schedule for the week.

00:03:17.140 --> 00:03:20.820
You might need a parent to assist if you are under 18 for the download.

00:03:21.660 --> 00:03:27.220
Next is a reminder about using chatbots like chat GPT or Claude at school or work.

00:03:27.860 --> 00:03:32.600
One person shared that they use these tools every day but always double check the answers because

00:03:32.600 --> 00:03:39.240
small errors can hide and otherwise perfect looking text. That is a smart habit because AI gives a

00:03:39.240 --> 00:03:46.180
strong starting point, not the final answer every time. Try this experiment yourself. Open chat GPT

00:03:46.180 --> 00:03:52.460
or Claude and ask it to explain a topic you are studying right now. Then compare what it says to

00:03:52.460 --> 00:03:58.760
your textbook or a trusted site like Khan Academy. Notice which parts add helpful examples and where

00:03:58.760 --> 00:04:05.180
it might miss important details. Time for some quick bits. Google announced plans to put back more

00:04:05.180 --> 00:04:11.840
water than its data centers used by 2030 through new conservation projects. It is a direct response

00:04:11.840 --> 00:04:18.660
to worries about how much water large AI systems need. In another story, meta work with Microsoft,

00:04:19.240 --> 00:04:24.160
SpaceX, and law enforcement to shut down over a million fake accounts used for scams.

00:04:25.020 --> 00:04:30.300
Many of those accounts were linked to networks in Southeast Asia and AI helped spot the patterns

00:04:30.300 --> 00:04:36.820
that humans might have missed. That is it for today. Remember, every AI expert started exactly where

00:04:36.820 --> 00:04:42.680
you are right now. If something we talked about today made you curious, go try it. That is literally

00:04:42.680 --> 00:04:48.800
how learning works. Stay curious, keep experimenting, and we will see you tomorrow. If you would rather

00:04:48.800 --> 00:04:55.620
watch than listen, find us on YouTube at Nerra Network, links in the show notes. Before you go,

